{"name":"New Toile","hex":"#90abbb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"new","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Newar."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Recently made, or created.","Of recent origin; having taken place recently."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/new"},{"term":"toile","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plain or simple twilled fabric","a draft garment made of inexpensive cloth for the purposes of fitting and design evaluation"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/toile"}],"entries":[{"title":"Toile","blocks":[{"text":"Toile is a textile fabric comparable to fine batiste with a cloth weave. Natural silk or chemical fiber filaments are usually used as materials. The word toile can refer to the fabric itself or to a test garment sewn from calico. The French term toile entered the English language around the 12th century, was used in the Middle Ages and has since disappeared."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Handkerchief.jpg/330px-Handkerchief.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toile","wikidata":"Q1422692"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Printeddress.jpg/500px-Printeddress.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Printeddress.jpg/500px-Printeddress.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Dress from Toile de Jouy","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APrinteddress.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Le_studio_d%27Yves_Saint_Laurent_%28Mus%C3%A9e_Yves_Saint_Laurent%2C_Paris%29_-_31940295547.jpg/500px-Le_studio_d%27Yves_Saint_Laurent_%28Mus%C3%A9e_Yves_Saint_Laurent%2C_Paris%29_-_31940295547.jpg?utm